tasks.acceptance_prose:
GOAL (spec/test gap on behaviour the whole campaign system depends on): the dispatch tie-break by task `priority` is REAL but UNSPECIFIED and UNTESTED. `src/scheduler.rs::ready_tasks` orders ready tasks by deeper critical-path depth first, then `priority.rank()` DESCENDING, then original config order:

    indexed.sort_by(|(ia, a), (ib, b)| {
        let da = depths.get(&a.id)...; let db = depths.get(&b.id)...;
        db.cmp(&da).then(b.priority.rank().cmp(&a.priority.rank())).then(ia.cmp(ib))
    });

That second key is what makes campaign design work at all - it is how a task is made to go first among equally deep ready tasks - and it is exactly the mechanism that round 8 silently relied on and mis-set (priority 2 dispatches before priority 1; higher rank wins). Nothing in `openspec/specs/scheduling/spec.md` mentions it: the `Critical-path priority` requirement only says deeper tasks go first, and no test anywhere asserts the dispatch ORDER produced by priority. Existing coverage is only `tests/cli.rs::priority_levels_parse_and_rank_deterministically` (parsing/rank values) and a comment in `tests/contract_scheduler.rs` about the equal-priority fallback.

REQUIRED WORK:
1. Add a scenario to the `Critical-path priority` requirement in `openspec/specs/scheduling/spec.md` using EXACTLY this title so the traceability marker can reference it: `#### Scenario: priority breaks ties among equally deep ready tasks`. Precise meaning: GIVEN several ready tasks at the SAME dependency depth with different `priority` ranks and no scope overlap; WHEN the scheduler orders them for dispatch; THEN the higher rank is dispatched first, and tasks with EQUAL rank keep their original order from the config file. Write it in the GIVEN/WHEN/THEN style of the rest of the library. CRITICAL: a `## MODIFIED Requirements` block is a FULL REPLACEMENT, not a patch - reproduce the existing `deeper task first` scenario VERBATIM alongside the new one, or it is silently deleted.
2. Add `#[test] fn priority_breaks_ties_among_equally_deep_ready_tasks()` to `tests/contract_scheduler.rs` (the existing home of scheduler unit-level tests; it already builds `Config` values directly and exercises `ready_tasks`). Assert BOTH halves: strictly higher rank orders first, and equal rank preserves the original config order (which is the documented fallback, so prove it rather than assume it). Use at least three tasks at equal depth with mixed ranks, and include the numeric-vs-string `priority` forms if that is easy.
3. Do NOT change `src/scheduler.rs`. This task fixes the specification and the missing test, not the behaviour - if your test shows the behaviour differs from what you documented, STOP and report that in your final message instead of silently editing the scheduler.

TRACEABILITY: put `// spec: scheduling/<req>#priority-breaks-ties-among-equally-deep-ready-tasks` on the new test. `cargo test --test spec_traceability` must stay green and the allowlist in `tests/spec_traceability.rs` must stay empty (this is the whole point - a scenario is only acceptable if a test references it).
DO NOT BREAK: `tests/contract_scheduler.rs`'s existing tests, `tests/cli.rs`'s scheduler tests, and the scenario count of the scheduling spec (you add one scenario to an existing requirement - report the before/after requirement and scenario counts in your final message).
